---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much does a handyman cost in the Nashville area?',
    a: 'Handyman work in Middle Tennessee typically runs $60 to $125 per hour, or flat rates by job. Many have a one to two hour minimum or a minimum service charge, often around $100 to $150, so a single tiny task rarely pencils out on its own.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do handymen charge a minimum?',
    a: 'Drive time, fuel, and scheduling cost the same whether the job takes 20 minutes or two hours. A minimum covers the trip. It is also why bundling several small jobs into one visit is far cheaper per task than calling for each one separately.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a handyman cheaper than a specialized contractor?',
    a: 'For small, general repairs, yes. A handyman handles a mixed punch list in one visit at one rate. For licensed trade work at scale, like a full re-pipe or a panel replacement, a specialist is the right call. The savings come from not hiring four specialists for a list a single crew could clear.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="handyman-cost-middle-tennessee"
  eyebrow="Cost Guide"
  title="What Does a Handyman Cost in Middle Tennessee?"
  description="Typical handyman pricing in the Nashville area: hourly rates, flat-rate jobs, minimum fees, and why bundling small repairs into one visit saves money."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Handyman work in the Nashville area typically runs <strong>$60 to $125 per
    hour</strong>, or a flat rate by job, with most outfits holding a
    <strong>minimum service charge around $100 to $150</strong>. That minimum
    is the key to spending less: the trip costs the same whether you book one
    task or ten, so the cheapest way to buy handyman work is to bundle it.
    Here is how the pricing actually breaks down.
  </p>

  <h2>The three ways handyman work gets priced</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hourly ($60 – $125/hr):</strong> common for mixed or open-ended jobs where the scope is not clear until someone is on site.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat rate by job:</strong> common for known tasks, a faucet swap, a ceiling fan install, a few drywall patches, where the price can be quoted up front.</li>
    <li><strong>Minimum service charge ($100 – $150):</strong> the floor for a visit, covering drive time and setup regardless of how small the task is.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What common small jobs typically cost</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical range</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Faucet replacement</td><td>$120 – $250</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ceiling fan install</td><td>$120 – $300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toilet replacement</td><td>$150 – $350</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drywall patch (small)</td><td>$100 – $250</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interior door install</td><td>$150 – $350</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Light fixture swap</td><td>$100 – $200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Caulk / re-seal a bathroom</td><td>$100 – $200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mount TV / shelving</td><td>$100 – $250</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Ranges depend on parts, access, and how many jobs share the visit. The
    single item at the top of a list costs more per task than the same item
    when it is one of six.
  </p>

  <h2>Why bundling is the real savings</h2>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      Because the minimum covers the trip, one visit that clears a whole punch
      list is far cheaper per task than six separate calls. A sticking door,
      a running toilet, two drywall patches, a loose railing, and a dead
      outlet is one visit, not five. This is the entire logic behind a general
      maintenance crew: one call, one trip, the whole list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Handyman vs. specialized trade</h2>
  <p>
    For a mixed list of small repairs, a general handyman or maintenance crew
    is the cheaper and faster choice, one rate, one visit. For heavy licensed
    work, a full re-pipe, a roof replacement, an electrical panel, you want a
    specialist. The waste happens when owners hire four specialists for a list
    one crew could have cleared, paying four trip charges and coordinating
    four schedules. Upkeep covers <a href="/services/general-maintenance/">general
    maintenance</a> and the specialized trades under one roof, so the punch
    list and the big job both go through one point of contact.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Expect $60 to $125 an hour or a flat job rate, plan around the minimum,
    and bundle small jobs into one visit to get the most for the trip charge.
    Upkeep runs general repairs and punch lists across Nashville, Brentwood,
    Franklin, and the rest of Middle Tennessee, flat-rate and documented. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> with your list gets a quote.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
